Falsifies `system_tests::user::irq_send_refusal_tests::a_send_to_an_interrupts_endpoint_is_refused_and_the_driver_never_sees_it`.

The defect: `Rendezvous::send` forgets DECISIONS §101 (notification objects)'s ruling B and decides as it did before it.
`bind_irq` still marks the endpoint and nothing reads the mark, so the kernel compiles, every
`Send::Refused` arm is simply never reached, and every other test stays green.

Expected to fail: the `SEND` assertion, which gets `Ok(0)` where it wants `NotPermitted`,
because the send rendezvouses with the driver parked in `Irq::WAIT`. That driver wakes holding
`1`, which is exactly what its interrupt looks like.

